window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-1", "source": "2023年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on a campus event that has impressed them most. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Among all the campus events I have taken part in, a volunteer teaching trip impressed me the most. It was last\nautumn that I joined our university volunteer team and traveled to a small rural primary school.\nI still remember the moment when I walked into that simple classroom and began my first English lesson. The\nchildren sat straight, their eyes shining with curiosity, and they repeated every new word after me as loudly as\nthey could. When the bell rang, instead of running out to play, they crowded around my desk, asking how to\npronounce the words on their notebooks. At that moment, I felt truly needed for the first time, and a warm\nsense of responsibility rose in my heart.\nThis experience taught me that knowledge is not only something for exams, but also a light that can warm and\nguide others. That is exactly why this volunteer trip remains the campus event that touches me most."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国政府十分重视人民的健康饮食(diet)。通过大力提倡健康饮食，人们对合理营养增进健康的重要性有了更加深刻的认识。\"吃得安全、吃得营养、吃得健康\"是人民对美好生活的基本需要，是提升人民幸福感的必然要求，也为食品产业的发展提供了新机遇。目前，各级政府都在采取多种举措确保人民饮食健康，推进健康中国的建设。", "sample": "The Chinese government attaches great importance to people's healthy diet. Through the vigorous promotion of a healthy diet, people have come to better understand the importance of balanced nutrition in improving health. Eating safely, nutritiously and healthily is a basic need of the people for a better life, an inevitable requirement for enhancing their sense of happiness, and also offers new opportunities for the development of the food industry. At present, governments at all levels are taking various measures to ensure people's dietary health and promote the building of a Healthy China."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2023-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Exercising for just 10 minutes a week is linked to a longer life, according to a new study published in The British Journal of Sports Medicine.\nSeveral recent studies have found that even low-intensity exercise, done for a short amount of time, can have a meaningful  __26__  on health. Still, the idea that exercising for just 10 minutes a week may be enough to increase your lifespan is novel. It's also somewhat  __27__  , since the federal physical activity guidelines recommend getting at least 75 minutes of vigorous exercise or 150 minutes of  __28__  exercise each week.\nThe study was based on data from more than 88,000 U.S. adults who  __29__  in the National Health Interview Survey between 1997 and 2008.\nContrary to some research that has found an  __30__  limit to the amount of exercise that is healthy, the researchers found that there was  __31__  no limit to the longevity(长寿) benefits of exercise. Even the small group of people who got 10 times the amount of exercise recommended by the federal government had a 46% lower  __32__  of death than the least active group.\nStill, observational studies like this one cannot prove cause and effect; they can only find  __33__  . The researchers also were not able to  __34__  for certain lifestyle factors that could affect lifespan, including dietary habits and changes in physical activity over time. Despite these  __35__  , the study's results are yet another indication of the power of physical activity, even in small amounts.", "matchIntro": "How Climate Change Will Affect What You Eat", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Earlier this year, scientists warned that one in six animal species could go extinct(灭绝的) due to climate change. Could the same thing happen to our crops and other foodstuffs too?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It's clear that farmers in many parts of the world are going to find things harder in the coming decades. Last week, BBC Future explored one scientist's efforts to help crops cope with the increased probability of droughts. By using the genes from resurrection(复活) plants, Jill Farrant of the University of Cape Town is exploring whether she can design crops to survive for much longer periods without water."}, {"label": "C", "text": "But if we can't find ways to protect other foods, will they survive climate change? Fortunately, there is some good news on this front. Despite alarmist headlines about \"foods that are going extinct,\" there is no evidence that major food types like beans, chocolate, wine, corn or wheat will cease to exist."}, {"label": "D", "text": "But that doesn't mean it's all good news for future food. We will probably have to change where we grow certain crops, as some regions get too hot. The disadvantage, obviously, is that local farmers will suffer under this situation. And some people may struggle to get the same access to certain foods. \"Even if overall food production may be unaffected, food security can still be impacted,\" says Margaret Walsh, a scientist at the US Department of Agriculture's Climate Change Program Office. In other words, even if a certain food is still grown on some corner of the Earth, it doesn't mean that everyone will continue to have the same degree of access as today."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Overall, the yields of many foods, from staples to life-enhancing extras such as coffee and chocolate, will likely be impacted by climate change too. How those decreases will be felt will depend on the degree of warming and the crop in question, but in general, \"anything over about 30℃ is very bad for crops,\" says Wolfram Schlenker, an associate professor of international and public affairs at Columbia University. For example, statistical studies that he and a colleague built of corn and soybean(大豆) production in the US show a steep decline after crossing the 30℃ temperature threshold(临界点)."}, {"label": "F", "text": "In the US—the world's largest producer of corn and soybeans—farms can move north to some degree, Schlenker says. But eventually, yields will likely suffer because the soil north of Iowa declines in quality—a legacy of glacial(冰川的) expansion. Other studies, including studies of wheat in India and corn in Africa, also found that there is a threshold above which yields sharply decline: crops can adapt and move, but only to a point. \"What's common to all studies is the finding that extreme heat is damaging to crop growth, although exact cutoffs vary by crop,\" Schlenker says. \"If predictions for the end of the century are true, though, I think a lot of agricultural areas in the US will see significant hits.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Under current conditions, about 4% of the world's croplands experience drought in any given year, but by the end of the century those conditions are forecasted to jump to about 18% per year. Some studies indicate that horticulture crops—generally, everything besides staples—may be impacted most severely, largely because they tend to be confined to a smaller geographic area. Researcher Andrew Jarvis and his colleagues found that 80% of coffee-growing zones in Central America and Brazil could become unsuitable by 2050, for example, while climate change will likely have \"great impacts\" on cocoa(可可粉) production in West Africa. \"High quality chocolate will be less available in the future, and if you want it, you'll have to pay a lot more for it,\" Jarvis says."}, {"label": "H", "text": "This means that, for those who can afford it, some foods will simply cost them more in the future. But for poorer people, those same price jumps will likely cause certain foods to go extinct from their diets. \"The more you reduce, the shorter the supply, and the higher the price will jump,\" Schlenker says."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Another potential climate change-induced problem is our dependence on commodity crops—wheat, soybeans, corn and rice—which currently provide humanity with 75% of its calories, either directly or indirectly through the animals we raise on those crops. Jarvis and his colleagues also found that, over the past five decades, the world has seen an increasing standardisation of diets; the foods we eat globally today are 36% more similar than they were in 1961. While this can be good news for the world's poorest people who now consume more calories, protein and fat than in the past, homogeneity(同一性) and over-dependence on a handful of staples leave us vulnerable to threats such as drought, disease and pests—all of which are predicted to worsen in many parts of the world as a result of climate change."}, {"label": "J", "text": "There are ways we could soften the coming blow to the global food supply, however. Like Farrant's work with resurrection crops, a number of companies, organisations and researchers are aiming to create drought and temperature-resistant crops through genetic engineering and conventional breeding. For now, the jury is still out as to how successful those endeavours will be. \"The people at Monsanto who I've talked to are much more optimistic that they'll be able to engineer heat-tolerant crops,\" Schlenker says. \"On the other hand, scientists at the USDA who I've spoken with are much more cautious.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Until genetic engineering comes to fruition, other strategies might also help in some places, including applying more fertiliser, implementing better irrigation, using machinery that gets crops out of the field faster or installing storage facilities to delay spoilage. \"Many places could benefit a great deal just by using technologies that already exist,\" Walsh says. \"General farm management can go a long way toward easing changes.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Finally, diversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other crops could also help. \"We've seen profound changes in the last decades in what we eat largely as a result of international trade, and I think that trend toward more diversification will continue,\" M Jarvis says. \"Depending on a greater number of plant species creates a more vigorous and less risky food system—and one that provides a broader range of nutritional requirements.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "One of my bad habits is saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I'm doing. Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm supposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say. But working long hours doesn't drive better results. Never taking a vacation won't lead to a promotion. So why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are all the time?\nIn 2016, researchers from Columbia, Harvard, and Georgetown conducted a study to figure it out. They found busy people are perceived to be of high status, and interestingly, these status attributions are heavily influenced by our own beliefs about social mobility. In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of higher standing.\nThat's why we feel like we have to appear busy, and there's a real perception that if someone is knee-deep in meetings, emails, and stress, then they're probably a big deal. This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According to a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\nPersonally, I'm going to stop saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I am. It sounds self-righteous(自以为是的) and sets the wrong tone. Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound like you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your time off.\nThat's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use before your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on the weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same.", "47": "One of my bad habits is saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I'm doing. Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm supposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say. But working long hours doesn't drive better results. Never taking a vacation won't lead to a promotion. So why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are all the time?\nIn 2016, researchers from Columbia, Harvard, and Georgetown conducted a study to figure it out. They found busy people are perceived to be of high status, and interestingly, these status attributions are heavily influenced by our own beliefs about social mobility. In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of higher standing.\nThat's why we feel like we have to appear busy, and there's a real perception that if someone is knee-deep in meetings, emails, and stress, then they're probably a big deal. This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According to a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\nPersonally, I'm going to stop saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I am. It sounds self-righteous(自以为是的) and sets the wrong tone. Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound like you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your time off.\nThat's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use before your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on the weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same.", "48": "One of my bad habits is saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I'm doing. Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm supposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say. But working long hours doesn't drive better results. Never taking a vacation won't lead to a promotion. So why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are all the time?\nIn 2016, researchers from Columbia, Harvard, and Georgetown conducted a study to figure it out. They found busy people are perceived to be of high status, and interestingly, these status attributions are heavily influenced by our own beliefs about social mobility. In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of higher standing.\nThat's why we feel like we have to appear busy, and there's a real perception that if someone is knee-deep in meetings, emails, and stress, then they're probably a big deal. This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According to a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\nPersonally, I'm going to stop saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I am. It sounds self-righteous(自以为是的) and sets the wrong tone. Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound like you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your time off.\nThat's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use before your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on the weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same.", "49": "One of my bad habits is saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I'm doing. Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm supposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say. But working long hours doesn't drive better results. Never taking a vacation won't lead to a promotion. So why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are all the time?\nIn 2016, researchers from Columbia, Harvard, and Georgetown conducted a study to figure it out. They found busy people are perceived to be of high status, and interestingly, these status attributions are heavily influenced by our own beliefs about social mobility. In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of higher standing.\nThat's why we feel like we have to appear busy, and there's a real perception that if someone is knee-deep in meetings, emails, and stress, then they're probably a big deal. This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According to a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\nPersonally, I'm going to stop saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I am. It sounds self-righteous(自以为是的) and sets the wrong tone. Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound like you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your time off.\nThat's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use before your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on the weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same.", "50": "One of my bad habits is saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I'm doing. Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm supposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say. But working long hours doesn't drive better results. Never taking a vacation won't lead to a promotion. So why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are all the time?\nIn 2016, researchers from Columbia, Harvard, and Georgetown conducted a study to figure it out. They found busy people are perceived to be of high status, and interestingly, these status attributions are heavily influenced by our own beliefs about social mobility. In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of higher standing.\nThat's why we feel like we have to appear busy, and there's a real perception that if someone is knee-deep in meetings, emails, and stress, then they're probably a big deal. This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According to a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\nPersonally, I'm going to stop saying \"busy\" when people ask me how I am. It sounds self-righteous(自以为是的) and sets the wrong tone. Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound like you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your time off.\nThat's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use before your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on the weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same.", "51": "Female employees consistently pay lower airfares than men do for the same flights because they tend to book earlier.\nWe compared the airfare paid by employees in the same position within a company for the same class of travel and used a common statistical technique to account for other factors that might affect differences in airfares. We found that women paid on average $18 less per ticket than their male colleagues. Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\nWe wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips so we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or male frequent travelers being inclined to book late. None of these explained away the gender gap, so we applied data collected from surveys that express consumer preferences that play a central role in economic decisions, such as patience and risk avoidance.\nWe found that only the concept of \"negative reciprocity\"—in which an employee who feels unfairly treated engages in negative behaviors, such as spending their company's money less carefully—explains these differences. The surveys showed men tend to exhibit more of these negative behaviors than women. This isn't to say that all men engage in these behaviors—or that booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal behavior. It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative reciprocity variable.\nPrior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\nOur results show another way these negative behaviors can manifest themselves, like in airline bookings, and add to evidence that women are less likely to engage in them.\nCompanies spend significant sums of money on business travel. While that $18 difference per ticket may seem small, it adds up. Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year for a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers.", "52": "Female employees consistently pay lower airfares than men do for the same flights because they tend to book earlier.\nWe compared the airfare paid by employees in the same position within a company for the same class of travel and used a common statistical technique to account for other factors that might affect differences in airfares. We found that women paid on average $18 less per ticket than their male colleagues. Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\nWe wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips so we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or male frequent travelers being inclined to book late. None of these explained away the gender gap, so we applied data collected from surveys that express consumer preferences that play a central role in economic decisions, such as patience and risk avoidance.\nWe found that only the concept of \"negative reciprocity\"—in which an employee who feels unfairly treated engages in negative behaviors, such as spending their company's money less carefully—explains these differences. The surveys showed men tend to exhibit more of these negative behaviors than women. This isn't to say that all men engage in these behaviors—or that booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal behavior. It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative reciprocity variable.\nPrior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\nOur results show another way these negative behaviors can manifest themselves, like in airline bookings, and add to evidence that women are less likely to engage in them.\nCompanies spend significant sums of money on business travel. While that $18 difference per ticket may seem small, it adds up. Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year for a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers.", "53": "Female employees consistently pay lower airfares than men do for the same flights because they tend to book earlier.\nWe compared the airfare paid by employees in the same position within a company for the same class of travel and used a common statistical technique to account for other factors that might affect differences in airfares. We found that women paid on average $18 less per ticket than their male colleagues. Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\nWe wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips so we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or male frequent travelers being inclined to book late. None of these explained away the gender gap, so we applied data collected from surveys that express consumer preferences that play a central role in economic decisions, such as patience and risk avoidance.\nWe found that only the concept of \"negative reciprocity\"—in which an employee who feels unfairly treated engages in negative behaviors, such as spending their company's money less carefully—explains these differences. The surveys showed men tend to exhibit more of these negative behaviors than women. This isn't to say that all men engage in these behaviors—or that booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal behavior. It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative reciprocity variable.\nPrior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\nOur results show another way these negative behaviors can manifest themselves, like in airline bookings, and add to evidence that women are less likely to engage in them.\nCompanies spend significant sums of money on business travel. While that $18 difference per ticket may seem small, it adds up. Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year for a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers.", "54": "Female employees consistently pay lower airfares than men do for the same flights because they tend to book earlier.\nWe compared the airfare paid by employees in the same position within a company for the same class of travel and used a common statistical technique to account for other factors that might affect differences in airfares. We found that women paid on average $18 less per ticket than their male colleagues. Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\nWe wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips so we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or male frequent travelers being inclined to book late. None of these explained away the gender gap, so we applied data collected from surveys that express consumer preferences that play a central role in economic decisions, such as patience and risk avoidance.\nWe found that only the concept of \"negative reciprocity\"—in which an employee who feels unfairly treated engages in negative behaviors, such as spending their company's money less carefully—explains these differences. The surveys showed men tend to exhibit more of these negative behaviors than women. This isn't to say that all men engage in these behaviors—or that booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal behavior. It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative reciprocity variable.\nPrior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\nOur results show another way these negative behaviors can manifest themselves, like in airline bookings, and add to evidence that women are less likely to engage in them.\nCompanies spend significant sums of money on business travel. While that $18 difference per ticket may seem small, it adds up. Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year for a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers.", "55": "Female employees consistently pay lower airfares than men do for the same flights because they tend to book earlier.\nWe compared the airfare paid by employees in the same position within a company for the same class of travel and used a common statistical technique to account for other factors that might affect differences in airfares. We found that women paid on average $18 less per ticket than their male colleagues. Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\nWe wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips so we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or male frequent travelers being inclined to book late. None of these explained away the gender gap, so we applied data collected from surveys that express consumer preferences that play a central role in economic decisions, such as patience and risk avoidance.\nWe found that only the concept of \"negative reciprocity\"—in which an employee who feels unfairly treated engages in negative behaviors, such as spending their company's money less carefully—explains these differences. The surveys showed men tend to exhibit more of these negative behaviors than women. This isn't to say that all men engage in these behaviors—or that booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal behavior. It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative reciprocity variable.\nPrior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\nOur results show another way these negative behaviors can manifest themselves, like in airline bookings, and add to evidence that women are less likely to engage in them.\nCompanies spend significant sums of money on business travel. While that $18 difference per ticket may seem small, it adds up. Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year for a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What may be the reason that friends are similar to each other according to the recent research?", "options": ["A) Their brains work in harmony.", "B) They are generally the same age.", "C) Their interests are quite similar.", "D) They have the same ethnic background."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Their brains work in harmony.\n【定位】It may be because your brains operate in harmony with each other.\n这可能是因为你们的大脑彼此协同运作。\n【信号】因果信号 because。录音开头先抛问句“有没有发现你和朋友很像“，紧接 It may be\nbecause your brains operate in harmony with each other —— because 就是把答案点\n亮的因果信号词，because 后面那句正是“相似的原因“，直接命中题干所问。\n【替换】your brains operate in harmony with each other ↔ Their brains work in harmony.\noperate 换说成 work\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 friends are more likely to be the same age，They are generally the\nsame age. 正盗用了这个 same age。但这句前面有 We know that，是“早已知道的旧结论\n“，并非题干所问的“近期研究发现的相似原因“——原因落在大脑那句，不在同龄这句。\n借用原文：friends are more likely to be the same age, gender and ethnic background\nC) 未提及\nTheir interests are quite similar. 听起来很合常识，但录音通篇没提“兴趣（interests）“相\n似，只说了年龄、性别、族裔背景与大脑。它靠的是考生“朋友兴趣相投“的生活常识，而非\n录音内容，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey have the same ethnic background. 盗用了录音的 ethnic background。和 B 一\n样，这句出自 We know that friends are more likely to be the same age, gender and\nethnic background，是“已知的旧情况“，不是近期研究新发现的相似原因，答非所问。\n借用原文：the same age, gender and ethnic background", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the news report say about the relationship between friends?", "options": ["A) It can be touching.", "B) It is hard to predict.", "C) It can work both ways.", "D) It resembles family ties."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It can work both ways.\n【定位】The relationship probably goes two ways. We are drawn to people who think like us; we then\ninfluence their thoughts over time, which may push their brain activity into more closely\nresembling our own.\n这种关系很可能是双向的。我们被与自己想法相近的人吸引；随后我们又会逐渐影响他们的\n想法，这可能促使他们的大脑活动变得更接近我们自己的。\n【信号】定性句 goes two ways。录音第二段结尾用 The relationship probably goes two ways\n直接给“关系“下定性，后面 We are drawn to people…; we then influence their\nthoughts 一来一回正是对“双向“的解释。这句“主语就是题干 relationship + 紧跟一个性质\n判断“的结构，就是答案句信号。\n【替换】The relationship probably goes two ways ↔ It can work both ways.\ntwo ways 换成 both ways\nWe are drawn to people who think like us; we then influence their thoughts ↔\nIt can work both ways.\n一来一回印证双向\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt can be touching. 盗用了录音的 touching，可那个 touching 出现在 Some people\nmight find a romantic scene touching——是在举例说明“不同人对同一画面反应不同“，\n说的是“浪漫场景“感人，根本不是在评价“朋友关系“。张冠李戴，答非所问。\n借用原文：Some people might find a romantic scene touching, for instance\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is hard to predict. 借了录音的 predict。但录音说的是 it could be used to predict\nwhether two people were already friends or not——意思是“大脑相似度高到能用来预测\n两人是不是朋友“，是说“能预测“，根本没说“关系难预测“。选项把“能预测“反着说成了“难预\n测“，与原文相反。\n借用原文：it could be used to predict whether two people were already friends or not\nD) 未提及\nIt resembles family ties. 听着像那么回事，但录音全程没把朋友关系比作“家庭纽带\n（family ties）“，只说了关系“双向“。它纯靠考生自行联想，录音无任何对应，属未提及。\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Two men who have been best friends for 60 years have just realized they are actually brothers. It's\nreported that Alan Robinson and Walter MacDonald met in the 6th grade at a school in Hawaii. They\nhave been best friends ever since. Alan was given away for adoption soon after he was born, and Walter\nnever knew his father. So recently, they made separate attempts on DNA matching websites to discover\nmore about their ancestry. Walter was astonished to find that he matched with website user Robi 737. He\nknew immediately that this was his best friend Alan, whose friends call him Robi and flew 737 airplanes\nwhen he was a pilot. The pair were born to the same mother 15 months apart.\n两名保持了60年最佳友谊的男子刚刚意识到他们实际上是兄弟。据报道，Alan Robinson和Walter\nMacDonald在夏威夷的一所学校读六年级时相识。从那时起他们就一直是最好的朋友。Alan出生后不久\n就被送去领养，而Walter从未见过自己的父亲。因此最近，他们分别在DNA匹配网站上尝试了解更多关\n于自己祖先的信息。Walter惊讶地发现他与网站用户Robi 737匹配。他立即知道这就是他最好的朋友\nAlan，Alan的朋友们都叫他Robi，而且他当飞行员时驾驶737飞机。这对朋友出生于同一位母亲，相隔\n15个月。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What did both Alan Robinson and Walter MacDonald attempt to do on DNA matching websites?", "options": ["A) Search for their half-brothers.", "B) Identify their biological fathers.", "C) Find out more about their ancestry.", "D) See whether they are actually related."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Find out more about their ancestry.\n【定位】So recently, they made separate attempts on DNA matching websites to discover more about\ntheir ancestry.\n所以最近，他们俩各自在DNA配对网站上做了尝试，想多了解一些自己的家世血统。\n【信号】题干原词attempt复现+目的句。题干问 attempt to do，录音里恰好出现 made separate\nattempts on DNA matching websites，attempt 和 DNA matching websites 原词对\n上，紧跟其后的 to discover more about their ancestry 用 to do 带出目的——这个‘题干\n关键词复现 + to do 目的句’就是答案信号。\n【替换】to discover more about their ancestry ↔ Find out more about their ancestry.\ndiscover换成find out，ancestry原样\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说去 Search for their half-brothers.(寻找同父异母的兄弟)。录音确实出现 they are\nactually brothers‘他们其实是兄弟’，A 盗用了 brothers 这个词，但录音里这是新闻最后揭\n晓的真相，不是他俩上网时‘想去找的目标’——而且他们是同母兄弟、不是 half-brothers，\n张冠李戴。\n借用原文：they are actually brothers\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 说去 Identify their biological fathers.(确认亲生父亲)。录音有 Walter never knew his\nfather，B 盗用了 father 一词，但这只是介绍背景，两人上网的目的写得很清楚是\ndiscover more about their ancestry(查家世)，不是专门去认父亲。\n借用原文：Walter never knew his father\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说去 See whether they are actually related.(看看俩人是否真有亲属关系)。录音里 they\nare actually brothers 用了 actually 一词，D 盗用了 actually 并改写成 related，可他们上\n网时压根不知道彼此有血缘、不是‘去验证是否有关系’，验出是兄弟是意外结果，不是上网\n动机。\n借用原文：they are actually brothers", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the news report say about Alan Robinson and Walter MacDonald?", "options": ["A) They were both given up for adoption.", "B) They were born to the same mother.", "C) They flew 737 airplanes as pilots.", "D) They were both 60 years of age."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They were born to the same mother.\n【定位】The pair were born to the same mother 15 months apart.\n这两个人是同一个母亲所生，出生时间相差15个月。\n【信号】结尾收束句给出血缘真相。新闻把最关键的血缘结论放在末句 The pair were born to the\nsame mother 15 months apart——新闻报道常把核心事实或结局压在结尾，这个‘篇末收\n束’位置就是答案信号，born to the same mother 与选项 B 直接对应。\n【替换】born to the same mother ↔ They were born to the same mother.\n原句几乎照搬主语换成They\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说两人 They were both given up for adoption.(都被送养)。录音里确有 Alan was\ngiven away for adoption，A 盗用了 adoption，但录音只说 Alan 一个人被送养，A 却说\n成 both(两个人都)，把范围从一人扩大到两人，与原文不符。\n借用原文：Alan was given away for adoption\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说两人 They flew 737 airplanes as pilots.(都当飞行员开737)。录音念到了 flew 737\nairplanes when he was a pilot，C 盗用了 737 和 pilot 这两个醒目词，但 he 指的只是\nAlan 一人，C 说成 They(两人都飞)，把一人之事安到两人头上。\n借用原文：flew 737 airplanes when he was a pilot\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说两人 They were both 60 years of age.(都60岁)。录音开头有 best friends for 60\nyears，D 盗用了数字 60，但这60是‘做了60年好朋友’，是友谊的年数，不是两人的年龄；\n偷换了60所修饰的对象。\n借用原文：best friends for 60 years", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "Why did one of the male tourists feel sad?", "options": ["A) One of his friends was caught littering.", "B) Other tourists refused to join in the cleanup.", "C) The beautiful beach was spoiled with lots of trash.", "D) The kilometer-long beach was practically deserted."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The beautiful beach was spoiled with lots of trash.\n【定位】He felt so sad to see such a gorgeous place littered with so much trash.\n他看到如此美丽的地方却被这么多垃圾弄得脏乱不堪，感到非常难过。\n【信号】feel sad 与题干同词直接命中。答案句直接出现题干里的 He felt so sad，紧跟其后的 to\nsee such a gorgeous place littered with so much trash 就是难过的原因；听到与题干情\n绪词同样的‘sad’，那一句就是答案句。\n【替换】such a gorgeous place littered with so much trash ↔\nThe beautiful beach was spoiled with lots of trash\n同义改写：美景被垃圾糟蹋\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项借用录音里的 his friends 一词，但原文只说他和朋友一起去捡垃圾，从没说‘朋友被抓\n乱扔垃圾’，是把出现过的原词拼成一个录音根本没说的情节。\n借用原文：he and his friends decided to grab a few trash bags\nB) 未提及\n录音说的是‘nobody was doing anything’（没人去管这些垃圾），并非‘其他游客拒绝加入\n清理’；‘refused to join in’这一情节录音从未出现，属凭空捏造。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项盗用录音里的 kilometer-long beach，却说海滩‘practically deserted（几乎空无一\n人）’；而录音强调的是海滩满是垃圾、不够美，与‘空无一人’语义不符，方向反了。\n借用原文：Yesterday two male and three female tourists were spotted walking the kilometer-\nlong beach", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Who put the good deed video on the internet?", "options": ["A) A passerby.", "B) A local woman.", "C) The beach authorities.", "D) One of the five tourists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A passerby.\n【定位】A man passing by decided to video their good deed and put it on the internet.\n一名路过的男子决定把他们的善举拍下来并发布到网上。\n【信号】动作 put it on the internet 与题干同动作。答案句出现与题干同样的动作 put it on the\ninternet，前面的主语 A man passing by 就是施动者；听到题干里的关键动作时，紧抓它\n的主语即可锁定答案。\n【替换】A man passing by ↔ A passerby\n短语压缩成单词\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nB 项盗用录音里的 One local woman，但这位本地女子做的是‘评论说自己很尴尬’，并非上\n传视频；把别人做的事安到她头上，属人物错位。\n借用原文：One local woman commented on being embarrassed that tourists were cleaning up\ntheir beach\nC) 未提及\n录音通篇没出现‘beach authorities（海滩管理方）’这个角色，上传视频的也不是任何官方\n机构，属凭空捏造的身份。\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 项盗用录音里的‘游客’，游客确实是事件主角、在捡垃圾，但发视频到网上的是路过男子\n而非游客中的某人；把上传动作错记到游客身上。\n借用原文：they were most likely traveling together", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why did one local woman say she was embarrassed?", "options": ["A) The tourists' good deed was not noticed by the locals.", "B) Some natives were selling poor-quality food to tourists.", "C) The number of tourists to the beach is on a steady decline.", "D) It was tourists not natives who were cleaning up the beach."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was tourists not natives who were cleaning up the beach.\n【定位】One local woman commented on being embarrassed that tourists were cleaning up\ntheir beach.\n一名本地女子评论说，她为游客在清理他们本地的海滩这件事感到尴尬。\n【信号】embarrassed that 直接引出原因。答案句出现题干同词 embarrassed，紧跟的 that\ntourists were cleaning up their beach 由 that 直接引出尴尬的原因；听到 embarrassed\n后那句 that 从句就是答案句。\n【替换】that tourists were cleaning up their beach ↔\nIt was tourists not natives who were cleaning up the beach\n补出隐含对照：游客而非本地人\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 项说‘游客善举没被本地人注意到’，但录音里本地女子明明注意到了才会尴尬，路人还拍\n了视频，恰恰说明被注意到了；‘not noticed’与录音事实相反。\n借用原文：One local woman commented on being embarrassed\nB) 偷换概念\nB 项盗用录音里的 food and drinks，但原文是说本地人‘带来食物饮料后留下垃圾’，绝非\n‘卖劣质食物给游客’；把‘带来食物乱丢垃圾’偷换成‘卖劣质食物’。\n借用原文：it was actually the natives who brought food and drinks then left trash all along\nthe island\nC) 未提及\n录音通篇没提‘游客数量在持续下降’这一信息，本题谈的是清理海滩与尴尬，与游客增减无\n关，属凭空捏造的全新话题。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why can't the man go for a run Saturday morning?", "options": ["A) He has to play football with workmates.", "B) He has got some books to read.", "C) He is going to visit a friend.", "D) He is physically unfit for it."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He is physically unfit for it.\n【定位】I'd love to, but I hurt my ankle last weekend playing football with work colleagues.\n我很想去，但我上周末和同事踢足球时弄伤了脚踝。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真理由。男士先说 I'd love to（很想去）是客套，紧跟的 but 才转出真正\n原因——I hurt my ankle，所以答案句落在 but 之后。\n【替换】I hurt my ankle ↔ He is physically unfit for it.\n具体伤情概括成\"身体不适合\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 He has to play football with workmates. 把原文里的 playing football with work\ncolleagues 原词搬来，但原文是说\"踢球时受了伤\"、不是\"周六要去踢球\"，听到 football\n就选会落进陷阱。\n借用原文：playing football with work colleagues\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 He has got some books to read. 说的是他后面提到的读书 I've been catching up\non some reading，那是第11题的内容，与\"为何不能去跑步\"无关，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I've been catching up on some reading\nC) 未提及\n选项 He is going to visit a friend. 在对话里根本没出现，男士不能去跑步的理由是脚伤，\n不是去看朋友，纯属编造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why is the woman's whole family going to the zoo next Saturday?", "options": ["A) To teach kids about animal protection.", "B) To learn how popular zoos could be.", "C) To see some rare animals in cages.", "D) To give her little nephew a treat."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To give her little nephew a treat.\n【定位】My little nephew turns six, and he loves the zoo, so the whole family is going.\n我的小侄子要满六岁了，他很喜欢动物园，所以全家一起去。\n【信号】因果 so 引出去的原因。so the whole family is going 里的 so 把前因\"小侄子过六岁生\n日、又喜欢动物园\"和后果\"全家去\"连起来，so 之前就是\"为什么去\"的答案。\n【替换】My little nephew turns six, and he loves the zoo ↔ To give her little nephew a treat.\n为侄子庆生=给侄子一份开心\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 To teach kids about animal protection. 借了女士后面说的 they are fun and\neducational for children 里的 educational，但原文只说动物园对孩子有趣又有教育意\n义，从没说全家去是为了\"教孩子动物保护\"，是把原词拔高到原文没说的目的。\n借用原文：they are fun and educational for children\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 To learn how popular zoos could be. 套用了 They are nowhere near as popular\nas they used to be 里的 popular，但那只是女士顺口评论动物园不如从前受欢迎，绝不是\n全家去的目的。\n借用原文：They are nowhere near as popular as they used to be\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 To see some rare animals in cages. 借了 the poor animals stuck in cages 里的\ncages，但原文是女士同情笼中动物，没说去看\"珍稀\"动物，rare 更是凭空添加。\n借用原文：the poor animals stuck in cages", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do we learn about Dan from the man's description?", "options": ["A) He enjoys excellent health.", "B) He is keen on extreme sports.", "C) He coaches tennis players every week.", "D) He spends most of his time in the gym."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He enjoys excellent health.\n【定位】Dan is extremely fit and healthy. He goes to the gym most days, plus tennis two or three times\na week.\nDan 非常健壮、健康。他几乎每天去健身房，外加每周打两三次网球。\n【信号】直接给出对 Dan 的总评。男士 Dan is extremely fit and healthy 是对 Dan 的直接定性评\n价，后面 gym、tennis 都是佐证，这句总评就是答案句。\n【替换】Dan is extremely fit and healthy ↔ He enjoys excellent health.\nextremely fit and healthy = excellent health\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 He is keen on extreme sports. 把 Dan 的运动夸大为\"极限运动\"，原文只说他常去健\n身房、打网球，从没提 extreme sports，属无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 He coaches tennis players every week. 搬来原文的 tennis 和 a week，但原文是\nDan 自己\"打\"网球 plus tennis two or three times a week，不是\"教\"网球选手，把\"打\"偷\n换成\"教\"。\n借用原文：plus tennis two or three times a week\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 He spends most of his time in the gym. 借了 He goes to the gym most days 里\n的 gym 和 most，但原文是\"多数日子去健身房\"，选项偷偷改成\"把大部分时间花在健身\n房\"，频率被夸大成时长。\n借用原文：He goes to the gym most days", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What has the man been doing since last weekend?", "options": ["A) Tending to his swollen ankle.", "B) Concentrating on reading.", "C) Writing three book reports.", "D) Planning Christmas celebrations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Concentrating on reading.\n【定位】I've been catching up on some reading. I was given three books over Christmas, and I'm only\nnow getting around to reading them.\n我一直在补读一些书。圣诞节别人送了我三本书，我现在才腾出空来读它们。\n【信号】I've been doing 直接应答。女士问他脚伤期间在干嘛，男士直接以 I've been catching\nup on some reading 应答，被问者的这句应答就是答案句，与题干时态对应。\n【替换】I've been catching up on some reading ↔ Concentrating on reading.\ncatching up on reading = concentrating on reading\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Tending to his swollen ankle. 借了女士问句里的 that swollen ankle，但脚踝肿只是\n他读书的背景，他并没说\"一直在护理脚踝\"，把背景说成正在做的活动。\n借用原文：that swollen ankle\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Writing three book reports. 搬来原文的 I was given three books 里的 three 和\nbooks，但原文是别人送他三本书\"读\"，不是他\"写三份读书报告\"，把\"读书\"偷换成\"写报\n告\"。\n借用原文：I was given three books over Christmas\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Planning Christmas celebrations. 借了 over Christmas 里的 Christmas，但原文\nChristmas 只是\"圣诞期间收到书\"的时间状语，他并没在\"筹备圣诞庆祝\"，纯属对原词的曲\n解。\n借用原文：over Christmas", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's book published recently?", "options": ["A) It is being debated by hundreds of retirees.", "B) It is attracting many people's attention.", "C) It partly records his own experience.", "D) It argues for postponing retirement."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is attracting many people's attention.\n【定位】John, your book is getting a lot of attention, partly because you write that most people can\nretire by 40.\n约翰，你的书正受到很多关注，部分原因是你在书里写道大多数人能在 40 岁退休。\n【信号】主持人开场对书的正面定性句。主持人介绍书时直接给出定性：your book is getting a lot\nof attention。这种\"主持人开场对作品的概括\"就是介绍类细节题的答案信号——它把书的\n核心特点一句点明。\n【替换】getting a lot of attention ↔ attracting many people's attention\n\"获得关注\"换说成\"吸引关注\"\n【排除】C) 偷换概念\n录音里作者说 I interviewed hundreds of people who retired by 40，是\"采访了几百\n人\"，并没说书\"记录他自己的经历\"；C 把\"研究别人\"偷换成\"写他自己\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：I interviewed hundreds of people who retired by 40\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说书主张 most people can retire by 40，是\"提前到 40 岁退休\"，而 D 说书主张\"推\n迟退休\"(postponing retirement)，正好反了。这是把\"提前\"听成\"推迟\"的反向陷阱。\n借用原文：most people can retire by 40\nA) 原词陷阱\n录音里 hundreds 来自 I interviewed hundreds of people，指作者\"采访了几百人\"，A 却\n说书\"正被几百名退休者讨论\"(debated by hundreds of retirees)，把\"被采访的人\"偷换\n成\"在讨论书的人\"，是听到 hundreds 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：I interviewed hundreds of people who retired by 40", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do the articles the woman read recommend?", "options": ["A) One should foresee a financial crisis.", "B) One should trust financial planners, figures.", "C) One should have one million dollars to retire.", "D) One should start saving as early as possible."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） One should have one million dollars to retire.\n【定位】I've read articles recommending one million dollars as a good figure for retirement.\n我读过一些文章，推荐一百万美元作为退休的合适数额。\n【信号】女士转述文章的 recommend 句。女士说 I've read articles recommending one million\ndollars，动词 recommend 与题干 recommend 完全对应，这句\"女士转述文章推荐什\n么\"就是答案信号——题目问的正是这个来源。\n【替换】one million dollars as a good figure for retirement ↔ have one million dollars to retire\n\"一百万作合适数额\"=\"应有一百万才退休\"\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nfinancial planners 确实在录音出现 most financial planners do give numbers，但那是\n嘉宾说\"规划师爱给数字、我觉得没用\"，文章并没\"推荐相信规划师\"。B 把原词搬来、又把\n内容安错了人(文章 vs 规划师)。\n借用原文：most financial planners do give numbers, I don't think that's useful\nD) 答非所问\n\"尽早储蓄\"听着像理财常识，但录音里文章推荐的是\"一百万这个数额\"，并没说\"尽早储\n蓄\"；至于储蓄、投资是嘉宾在后面(P14)讲的做法，不是\"女士读的文章\"推荐的。D 答非所\n问。\n借用原文：investing half your monthly income\nA) 未提及\n选项 A 的 financial crisis 借了话题里\"financial\"的语感，但文章推荐的是具体金额，从没\n让人\"预见金融危机\"；录音通篇也没出现\"危机\"的意思。A 是听到 financial 就联想出来的干\n扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say about retirement?", "options": ["A) It doesn't need to be permanent.", "B) It shouldn't be considered risky.", "C) It helps to reduce travel expenses.", "D) It is the way to quit a job one hates."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It doesn't need to be permanent.\n【定位】Retirement doesn't have to be permanent, and if people need to, they can go back to work.\n退休不一定是永久的，如果有需要，人们可以重新回去工作。\n【信号】男士对退休下的直接定性句。男士直接断言 Retirement doesn't have to be permanent，\n这是他对\"退休\"本身的一句正面定义，又用 they can go back to work 加以说明，是\"他怎\n么说退休\"的直接答案信号。\n【替换】Retirement doesn't have to be permanent ↔ It doesn't need to be permanent\ndoesn't have to=doesn't need to\nthey can go back to work ↔ doesn't need to be permanent\n\"能回去工作\"佐证\"不必永久\"\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nrisky 来自女士 That sounds risky to me 和男士 I counsel people to take risks——男士\n是\"劝人去冒险\"，承认有风险；B 却说退休\"不该被视为有风险\"，意思反了，是听到 risk 就\n想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：I counsel people to take risks\nD) 原词陷阱\na job they hate 确实出现在 spend their lives working on jobs they hate，但那是男士解\n释\"很多人因恐惧而困在烂工作里\"的背景，不是给退休下定义；D 把这句背景说成\"退休就\n是辞掉烂工作的途径\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：spend their lives working on jobs they hate\nC) 未提及\nC 说退休\"有助减少差旅开支\"(reduce travel expenses)，录音里 transport 出现在 cutting\nhousing, food, and transport expenses，那是\"怎样攒钱\"的省钱办法，与\"退休本身是什\n么\"无关，更没说退休能省路费。C 属嫁接他处原词的无中生有。\n借用原文：cutting housing, food, and transport expenses", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "How does the man say his proposal about retirement can be carried out?", "options": ["A) By keeping close contact with one's employers.", "B) By retiring when one reaches sixty years old.", "C) By investing half of one's monthly income.", "D) By following the counsel of financial planners."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By investing half of one's monthly income.\n【定位】Simple, by cutting housing, food, and transport expenses, and investing half your\nmonthly income.\n很简单，靠削减住房、饮食和交通开支，并拿出月收入的一半去投资。\n【信号】男士回答\"怎么做到\"的 by 操作句。面对女士\"怎么可能 40 岁退休\"的追问，男士直接答 by\ncutting housing, food, and transport expenses, and investing half your monthly\nincome，这句\"by + 一串具体动作\"就是 How 类题的答案信号。\n【替换】investing half your monthly income ↔ investing half of one's monthly income\n几乎原句对应的\"投月收入一半\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nemployers 出现在 you will be attracted to employers，那是男士讲\"保持技能就能被雇\n主青睐、好重返职场\"，回应的是\"能否回去工作\"，不是\"怎么攒钱提前退休\"；A 把它说\n成\"靠和雇主保持密切联系来实现退休\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：if you keep current with your skills, you will be attracted to employers\nB) 与原文相反\nsixty 来自女士的反问 most people can't retire at 60，意在质疑\"连 60 都退不了，怎么\n40 退\"；B 却说\"靠 60 岁退休来实现\"，方向反了——男士主张的是 40 岁提前退，不是等\n到 60。\n借用原文：how can anyone save enough to retire by 40 when most people can't retire at 60\nD) 与原文相反\nfinancial planners 和 counsel 都在录音里——但男士对规划师是 I don't think that's\nuseful，并不让人\"听规划师的\"；counsel 是他\"劝人冒险\"(I counsel people to take\nrisks)，不是\"听规划师劝告\"。D 把原词拼到一起，方向与男士立场相反。\n借用原文：While most financial planners do give numbers, I don't think that's useful", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What was the finding of the new research from America about consumers' food choices?", "options": ["A) They tended to be easily anticipated by those belonging to their own race.", "B) They tended to be arbitrarily judged by individuals of opposing groups.", "C) They were readily shared among members of the same social or racial group.", "D) They were influenced by the presence of someone from an outsider group."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They were influenced by the presence of someone from an outsider group.\n【定位】This research found college students were more likely to choose healthy food in the presence\nof an observer of a different race, as opposed to an observer from their own race.\n这项研究发现，与本族观察者在场相比，大学生在不同种族的观察者在场时更可能选择健康\n食物。\n【信号】结论标志词 found。录音 This research found 直接亮出研究结论，found 后面跟的就\n是研究发现，这是篇章交代核心结论的标志，听到 found/showed 这类词就知道答案句\n来了。\n【替换】in the presence of an observer of a different race ↔\ninfluenced by the presence of someone from an outsider group\n下位换上位\nwere more likely to choose healthy food ↔ were influenced\n详述换概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音后文 participants anticipated more negative judgment 里的 anticipated 一\n词，但把它扭成易被同族人预料，与研究发现毫无关系，是听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：participants anticipated more negative judgment\nB) 偷换概念\nB 说食物选择被对立群体的个人任意评判，借了录音 they felt less judged by members\nof their own group 的 judge 概念，但研究发现的是选择被在场者影响，不是选择本身被\n评判，偷换了概念。\n借用原文：they felt less judged by members of their own group\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说食物选择在同族群体内易被共享，但录音 they felt less judged by members of their\nown group 讲的是同族在场时人感觉评判更少、影响更小，根本没说共享选择，且方向与\n研究重点（外群体才带来改变）相反。\n借用原文：they felt less judged by members of their own group", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "When did 31 percent of students in the experiment select the healthier option?", "options": ["A) When they tried to make a positive impression on the researchers.", "B) When an unknown student from another university was present.", "C) When an experimenter from the research team took notice.", "D) When they were offered both candy and fruit as a snack."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） When an unknown student from another university was present.\n【定位】However, this number was 31 percent when in the presence of an unknown student from\nanother university.\n然而，当一名来自另一所大学的陌生学生在场时，这个数字是 31%。\n【信号】数字与 when 条件绑定。录音 this number was 31 percent when in the presence of an\nunknown student from another university 把 31 percent 与 when 引导的在场条件直接\n绑在一起，听到目标数字就顺势抓它后面的 when 从句，即答案条件。\n【替换】an unknown student from another university ↔ an unknown student from another university\n条件原句对应\nin the presence of ↔ was present\n名词短语换动词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说学生想给研究者留好印象，录音只说他们 use healthy food choices to make a\npositive impression，但针对的是外群体在场者，不是 researchers，录音从未提对研究者\n留好印象这个条件，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借了录音多次出现的 research/experimenter 概念，编出研究团队的实验员注意到时，但\n31 percent 绑定的是外校学生在场，与实验员留意无关，是听到 research 就想选的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：Other experiments showed similar results\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说同时提供糖果和水果作零食时，candy 和 fruit 确是录音 participants were offered\nthe choice between candy and fruit as a snack 的原词，但这是整个实验的设置，对应\n12% 与 31% 两种情境，不专属 31%，把背景条件错安成 31% 的专属触发，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：participants were offered the choice between candy and fruit as a snack", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "How could a healthy diet be promoted according to the findings of the new research?", "options": ["A) By advertising its social benefits.", "B) By teaching consumers diet strategies.", "C) By supporting struggling consumers.", "D) By maintaining its positive image."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By advertising its social benefits.\n【定位】This research finds that one way to promote a healthy diet could be to advertise the social\nbenefits of healthy choices.\n这项研究发现，推动健康饮食的一种方式可能是宣传健康选择的社会益处。\n【信号】建议句 one way to…could be to。录音 one way to promote a healthy diet could be\nto advertise the social benefits 用 one way to…could be to 这一建议句式给出对策，这\n是篇章结尾提对策的信号，听到就锁定后面的方法。\n【替换】advertise the social benefits of healthy choices ↔ advertising its social benefits\n动词变动名词\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 说教消费者饮食策略，录音虽提到 many attempts to help consumers make healthier\nchoices，但从未说具体方法是教饮食策略，这个手段是凭空添加的，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说支持苦苦挣扎的消费者，借了录音 consumers often struggle to maintain a healthy\ndiet 的 struggle 一词，但那只是描述现状，不是研究给出的对策，是听到原词就想选的陷\n阱。\n借用原文：consumers often struggle to maintain a healthy diet\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说维持其正面形象，借了录音 make a positive impression 里的 positive 概念，但那讲\n的是个人用健康选择给自己留好印象，不是推动健康饮食的对策，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：they strategically use healthy food choices to make a positive impression", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the recent study led by Patricia Pendry aim to examine?", "options": ["A) The academic and learning issues struggling students encounter.", "B) The risk students face due to a history of mental health problems.", "C) The effect of interacting with therapy dogs on students under pressure.", "D) The work universities are doing to help students succeed academically"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） The effect of interacting with therapy dogs on students under pressure.\n【定位】This study shows that traditional stress management approaches aren't as effective for this\npopulation compared with programs that focus on providing opportunities to interact with\ntherapy dogs.\n这项研究表明，对这一人群而言，传统的压力管理方法不如那些专注于提供与治疗犬互动机\n会的项目有效。\n【信号】总结句 This study shows。This study shows that 是篇章里的\"结论/点题\"信号——一\n旦听到\"这项研究表明……\"，后面紧跟的就是研究的核心发现/目的，这正是主旨题的答案\n落点。\n【替换】programs that focus on providing opportunities to interact with therapy dogs ↔\nThe effect of interacting with therapy dogs on students under pressure\n动作短语→名词化概括\nFor college students under pressure ↔ students under pressure\n几乎原词对应的对象\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了录音里的 academic and learning issues 原词，但那只是描述\"哪类学生处于风\n险\"的背景信息，不是研究要考察的内容。听到原词就选会中招。\n借用原文：academic and learning issues\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项搬用了录音的 a history of mental health issues，但那同样是在说明\"哪些学生有风\n险\"的铺垫，不是研究目的。把背景当主旨就错了。\n借用原文：a history of mental health issues\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项几乎照搬录音 Universities are doing a lot of great work trying to help students\nsucceed academically，但那是 Pendry 在铺垫\"大学在做很多帮扶工作\"的大背景，不\n是\"这项研究\"本身要考察的内容；研究聚焦的是治疗犬，不是泛泛的大学帮扶工作。\n借用原文：Universities are doing a lot of great work trying to help students succeed academically", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did the new study measure in the participants?", "options": ["A) Their communicative skills.", "B) Their executive functioning.", "C) Their academic networking.", "D) Their leadership capacities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Their executive functioning.\n【定位】The researchers measured executive functioning in the students involved in the study.\n研究人员测量了参与研究的学生的执行功能。\n【信号】原词 measured 直接领出宾语。录音用 The researchers measured executive\nfunctioning，measured 这个动词与题干 measure 完全对应，紧跟其后的宾语就是答案，\n是最直接的细节定位信号。\n【替换】measured executive functioning ↔ Their executive functioning\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\ncommunicative skills（沟通能力）录音从头到尾没提，是凭\"听起来像学生该有的能力\"硬\n编的干扰项，没有原文支撑。\nC) 原词陷阱\nacademic networking 把录音里多次出现的 academic（学业的）这个原词抓来拼了个新\n词，但录音讲的是 academic stress/success，从没说测\"学业人脉\"，是借原词 academic\n制造的假象。\n借用原文：academic stress management programs\nD) 未提及\nleadership capacities（领导力）录音里完全没出现，是又一个\"听上去像能力词\"的硬凑干\n扰，无原文依据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does Patricia Pendry think traditional stress management programs may do?", "options": ["A) Rid students of their anxiety.", "B) Add to some students' stress.", "C) Contribute little to typical students' success.", "D) Help students with mental issues pull through."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Add to some students' stress.\n【定位】But they're less helpful for struggling students. Pendry believes those students may\nexperience the programs as another lecture and feel even more stressed.\n但对学习困难的学生而言，这些项目帮助不大。Pendry 认为这些学生可能会把这些项目当\n成又一堂讲座，从而感到压力更大。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真实态度。But they're less helpful for struggling students 里的 But 是\n关键信号——前半句先肯定\"传统项目对普通学生有帮助\"，But 一转才说出 Pendry 真正的\n判断：对困难学生反而\"更有压力\"。态度题的答案几乎都在转折后。\n【替换】feel even more stressed ↔ Add to some students\\' stress\n感受描述→施动概括\nfor struggling students ↔ some students\n下位→上位概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项里的 anxiety 盗用了录音 manage stress or anxiety 的原词，且 Rid\\.\\.\\.of（彻底消\n除）把\"帮助管理焦虑\"夸大成\"消除焦虑\"，原文从没说传统项目能\"根除\"焦虑，程度被拔高\n了。\n借用原文：manage stress or anxiety\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明说传统项目 help typical students succeed——对普通学生是有帮助的；选项却\n说\"对普通学生的成功几乎没贡献\"，把\"有帮助\"反成了\"没帮助\"，正话反说，且对象\n（typical students）正是录音说有用的那群人。\n借用原文：these workshops help typical students succeed\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说传统项目对\"有困难的学生\" less helpful for struggling students——是帮不上忙；\n选项却说能\"帮有心理问题的学生渡过难关\"，把\"帮不上\"说成\"帮得上\"，方向反了，对象也\n被偷换成\"有心理问题的学生\"。\n借用原文：But they're less helpful for struggling students", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do entrepreneurs have to do when taking risks according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Work hard and plan carefully.", "B) Attempt to succeed at any cost.", "C) Aim high and expect great results.", "D) Remain optimistic even in difficulty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Work hard and plan carefully.\n【定位】On the contrary, taking risks in entrepreneurship involves careful planning and hard work.\n恰恰相反，创业中的冒险需要的是周密的规划和辛勤的努力。\n【信号】转折 On the contrary 后是真意。录音先说冒险“不是盲目去做、坐等大成果”，紧接着用\nOn the contrary 转折，转折之后那句 involves careful planning and hard work 才是正\n面答案；听到 On the contrary 就该锁定后半句。\n【替换】careful planning and hard work ↔ Work hard and plan carefully\n名词短语换成动词短语\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nAim high and expect great results 几乎照搬了录音被否定那半句的 expecting great\nresults，但录音是说“冒险”不等于“盲目坐等大成果”，这是被批评的做法，不是创业者“该\n做”的；听到 expect great results 就选，正好踩进陷阱。\n借用原文：expecting great results\nB) 未提及\nAttempt to succeed at any cost“不惜一切代价求成功”，录音通篇没说要“不计代价”，反\n而强调冒险要靠规划和努力、还提醒“并非所有风险都是好风险”，这种太满的说法是命题人\n添加的。\nD) 答非所问\nRemain optimistic even in difficulty 式的“乐观”确实在录音里出现过，an optimistic risk-\ntaker 也提到 optimistic，adapting in difficult times 也讲困境中应变，但那是讲“失败时\n的心态”，不是回答“冒险时必须做什么”这道题，把别处的内容搬来作答即错位。\n借用原文：an optimistic risk-taker", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the passage say is key to business growth?", "options": ["A) Regarding failure as something inevitable.", "B) Trying out innovative marketing strategies.", "C) Venturing into sectors never explored before.", "D) Being willing to experiment with novel ideas."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Being willing to experiment with novel ideas.\n【定位】The willingness to experiment with new ideas is key to business growth.\n愿意去尝试新点子，是企业成长的关键。\n【信号】原文 key 与题干同词对应。录音 is key to business growth 和题干 key to business\ngrowth 用词几乎一模一样，这句话主语 The willingness to experiment with new ideas\n就是答案；标志词 key 一响，定位即成。\n【替换】The willingness to experiment with new ideas ↔ Being willing to experiment with novel ideas\n近义改写 new=novel\n【排除】B) 未提及\nTrying out innovative marketing strategies 听起来很像“创新”，录音确有“new ideas”的\n意思，但根本没提“marketing strategies（营销策略）”；它用“创新”诱你，却塞了原文没有\n的“营销”。\nC) 过度推断\nVenturing into sectors never explored before“进入从未有人涉足的领域”比录音的“尝试\n新点子”程度重得多，录音只说 experiment with new ideas，并没说要闯入“全新行业”，这\n是把原意拔高了。\n借用原文：experiment with new ideas\nA) 原词陷阱\nRegarding failure as something inevitable 里 failure 是录音高频词，look at failure as\nan opportunity to learn 也讲了对失败的看法，但录音说的是“把失败当学习机会”，不是\n“把失败当不可避免”，更不是回答“增长关键”；它用熟悉的 failure 制造错觉。\n借用原文：look at failure as an opportunity to learn", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What are we advised to do when we fail?", "options": ["A) Expect future success so as to move forward.", "B) Learn from our failure and forge ahead.", "C) Distinguish between good and bad risks.", "D) Examine our strategies and find out weaknesses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Learn from our failure and forge ahead.\n【定位】But just remember that not all risks are good ones, and when you fail, learn from it and\nmove forward.\n但要记住，并非所有风险都是好风险；当你失败时，要从中吸取教训，然后继续前进。\n【信号】条件句 when you fail 直接应题。题干问 when we fail，录音正好有 when you fail, learn\nfrom it and move forward 这句条件句，措辞与题干对应；听到 when you fail 后面紧跟\n的 learn from it and move forward 就是答案。\n【替换】learn from it and move forward ↔ Learn from our failure and forge ahead\n代词还原＋近义短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nExpect future success so as to move forward 借用了录音的 move forward，但把前提\n偷换成“指望未来成功”——录音让你“前进”的前提是“从失败中学”，不是“指望成功”；听到\nmove forward 就选，正中陷阱。\n借用原文：move forward\nC) 答非所问\nDistinguish between good and bad risks 用了录音 not all risks are good ones 的意\n思，录音确实提醒“并非所有风险都好”，但那是另一层提醒，不是“失败时”被建议做的事，\n答非所问。\n借用原文：not all risks are good ones\nD) 未提及\nExamine our strategies and find out weaknesses“检查策略、找出弱点”录音没有对应说\n法，录音只讲“失败教你如何战略性思考”think and plan strategically，并没有让你去“查弱\n点”，是凭 strategically 一词联想出来的。\n借用原文：think and plan strategically", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does it mean to risk-taking businesses when most people tend to avoid risk?", "options": ["A) Fresher offers.", "B) Safer operation.", "C) More challenges.", "D) Less competition."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Less competition.\n【定位】Simply put, when most individuals stay away from risk, it means less competition for\nrisk-takers.\n简单地说，当大多数人远离风险时，对冒险者而言就意味着更少的竞争。\n【信号】Simply put 总结句给结论。录音用 Simply put（简而言之）领出一句总结，it means less\ncompetition for risk-takers 直接给出结论，与题干“what does it mean”正好对接；听到\nSimply put 这类收束词就知道结论要来了。\n【替换】less competition for risk-takers ↔ Less competition\n几乎原词截取\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFresher offers 借了录音 fresh offers 这个原词，但那句讲的是冒险者“用新点子、新供给、\n大胆发明在立标准”，是描述冒险者的做法，不是回答“别人避险意味着什么”，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：fresh offers\nC) 与原文相反\nMore challenges“更多挑战”与录音结论方向相反：录音说别人避险对冒险者是“更少竞争”\n这种好事，而 challenge 是偏负面的负担，录音的 adapting in difficult times 谈的是冒险\n者善应变，并没说“更多挑战”。\n借用原文：adapting in difficult times\nB) 未提及\nSafer operation 像是从录音 stay safe 联想来的，但 as long as you want to stay safe 是\n说“你若一味求稳就永远不会知道结果”，是对“守成者”的提醒，并不是说冒险企业能“更安全\n地运营”，原文没有这层意思。\n借用原文：stay safe", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) impact\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 meaningful 后、a 后缺名词\n【依据】have an impact on 是固定搭配，意为\"对……产生影响\"\neven low-intensity exercise, done for a short amount of time, can have a meaningful\n__(26)__ on health——have a(n) impact on health 即\"对健康有影响\"，搭配标准、语义\n贴切：低强度运动对健康有切实影响，正是本段要点。\n【竞争词】N) type type 不搭 meaningful…on，语义也不通\na meaningful type on health（\"对健康的一种有意义的类型\"）讲不通，type 也不与 on 构\n成\"对……的\"含义；这里要表达的是运动对健康的\"作用/影响\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) controversial\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 is + somewhat 之后作表语，需形容词\n【依据】因与官方指南冲突，故\"有争议\"\nIt's also somewhat __(27)__, since the federal physical activity guidelines recommend\ngetting at least 75 minutes of vigorous exercise or 150 minutes of __(28)__ exercise\n——since 给出理由：\"10分钟即可\"的说法和官方推荐的几十上百分钟相去甚远，自然\"有些\n争议\"。controversial 与这层让步/对立逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】H) moderate moderate 义为\"适度的\"，与 since 的争议逻辑不合，且它另有归宿\nmoderate 表\"中等/适度\"，无法解释\"because 它和官方指南相左\"这层因果；况且 moderate\n更贴 28 空的 moderate exercise（适度运动）固定搭配，应留给 28 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) moderate\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 exercise 前作定语，需形容词\n【依据】与 vigorous 对照，moderate exercise 即\"适度/中等强度运动\"\nat least 75 minutes of vigorous exercise or 150 minutes of __(28)__ exercise——\nvigorous（剧烈）对应 75 分钟，or 后强度更低的运动要更多时长 150 分钟，正是\nmoderate（中等强度）。moderate exercise 是健康指南里的固定表达，与 vigorous 形\n成强度对照。\n【竞争词】O) upper upper 不修饰运动强度，且它另有归宿\nupper 意为\"上面的/上限的\"，不能说 upper exercise；况且 upper 更贴 30 空的 upper limit\n（上限）固定搭配，应留给 30 题。这里要的是与 vigorous 对照的强度词。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) participated\n动词\n【词性槽】关系代词 who 后缺谓语，要填动词且用过去式\n【依据】participate in 是固定搭配，意为\"参与某调查\"\n空后紧跟 in：who __(29)__ in the National Health Interview Survey，participate in 正\n是\"参加（一项调查/活动）\"的标准搭配，主语是 88,000 名成年人，语义完全契合\"参加了\n全国健康访谈调查\"。\n【竞争词】A) adjust 搭配与语义都不对\nadjust 意为\"调整\"，常搭 adjust for/to，不能说\"人 adjust in 一项调查\"，语义上成年人\"调整进\n调查\"也讲不通。\nB) alter 义为\"改变\"，与\"参加调查\"无关\nalter 是\"改动、改变\"，\"成年人改变了这项调查\"既不合常理也不搭 in，与上下文的研究取样语境\n冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) upper\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 limit 前、an 后作定语，需形容词\n【依据】upper limit（上限）是固定搭配，呼应\"运动量过多是否仍健康\"\nContrary to some research that has found an __(30)__ limit to the amount of exercise\nthat is healthy——upper limit 即\"上限\"：有研究认为健康运动量存在一个上限，而本研究\n发现\"几乎没有上限\"，前后正成对比，upper limit 搭配地道、逻辑契合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) seemingly\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰 no limit 的程度/口吻，需副词\n【依据】对\"无上限\"这一惊人结论留有余地，\"看似\"无上限\nthere was __(31)__ no limit to the longevity benefits of exercise——\"运动延寿几乎没\n有上限\"是个强结论，作者用 seemingly（看似、貌似）留有余地，既转述发现又不把话说\n满，符合科普文严谨口吻。\n【竞争词】C) approximately approximately 修饰数字，不修饰\"no limit\"\napproximately\"大约\"通常修饰具体数量（approximately 75 minutes），用来修饰\"no\nlimit\"这种非数值判断不通顺；这里要的是表\"看似\"的语气副词，而非约数副词。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— approximately 词性也对，易与 seemingly 混淆。两者都\n是副词，there was __(31)__ no limit，考生可能想\"approximately no limit（大约没上\n限）\"；但 approximately 只修饰数字，修饰\"no limit\"这种结论不通。防坑：先问空修饰\n的是数字还是判断，约数副词只贴数字。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) risk\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 lower 之后、of death 之前缺名词\n【依据】risk of death（死亡风险）是固定搭配，且呼应\"延寿\"主题\nhad a 46% lower __(32)__ of death——risk of death 即\"死亡风险\"，运动多的人死亡风\n险低 46%，正好支撑全文\"运动有益寿命\"的主旨，搭配与语义双双契合。\n【竞争词】N) type type of death 不合此处逻辑\ntype of death 是\"死亡的类型\"，无法用\"lower\"来比较高低，也和\"运动延寿、降低死亡可能\"的\n语境对不上；这里要的是可量化升降的\"风险\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) patterns\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 find 后缺宾语，需复数名词\n【依据】与\"不能证因果、只能发现关联\"形成对照，patterns 即\"关联型态\"\nobservational studies like this one cannot prove cause and effect; they can only find\n__(33)__——分号前后是对比：不能证明因果，只能找出\"模式/关联\"。patterns 正指观察研\n究能看到的相关规律，与 cause and effect 恰成对照。\n【竞争词】E) coordinates 义为\"坐标\"，与研究方法语境无关\ncoordinates 多指数学\"坐标\"，放进\"观察研究只能发现什么\"的语境毫无意义，与 cause and\neffect 的对照逻辑也搭不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adjust\n动词\n【词性槽】情态 able to 之后缺动词原形\n【依据】adjust for 是研究术语，意为\"校正、排除……因素的影响\"\nwere not able to __(34)__ for certain lifestyle factors that could affect lifespan——\nadjust for（在统计中）\"对某些因素加以校正/控制\"是研究方法的固定说法，承接上文\"该研\n究有局限\"：研究者没能排除饮食、运动量变化等干扰因素，逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】B) alter alter 不搭 for，且语义相反\nalter 是\"改变\"，常作及物动词（alter something），不与 for 构成\"校正\"义；说\"研究者没能改\n变这些因素\"不合科研语境，这里要的是\"校正/控制\"而非\"改变\"。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— alter 与 adjust 同为\"改变\"义动词，极易误选。两词都是动\n词、词义都沾\"改动\"，考生易凭眼熟选 alter；但本句要的是科研术语 adjust for（校正/排\n除因素），alter 既不搭 for 又语义偏\"改变\"。防坑：动词空务必把空后的介词（for）和候\n选连读，搭不通的立刻淘汰。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adjust", "B) alter", "C) approximately", "D) controversial", "E) coordinates", "F) impact", "G) limitations", "H) moderate", "I) participated", "J) patterns", "K) populated", "L) risk", "M) seemingly", "N) type", "O) upper"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) limitations\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 Despite + these 之后缺名词，且要复数\n【依据】承接上文研究的两处不足，these 指代\"这些局限\"\n上文刚说研究 cannot prove cause and effect 又 were not able to __(34)__ for certain\nlifestyle factors，这些都是研究的不足；Despite these limitations（尽管有这些局限）正\n好回指这两处缺陷，转折后再肯定研究价值，逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】J) patterns 义为\"模式\"，指代不上前文的\"缺陷\"\npatterns 是\"模式、规律\"，前文讲的是研究做不到什么（局限），不是某种模式；说\"尽管有这\n些模式\"与上下文转折逻辑不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "One consequence of climate change is that some people may not have adequate access to certain foods.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】获取某些食物不充分\n【定位】And some people may struggle to get the same access to certain foods. \"Even if overall food\nproduction may be unaffected, food security can still be impacted,\" says Margaret Walsh, a\nscientist at the US Department of Agriculture's Climate Change Program Office.\n而且有些人可能难以再像从前那样获取某些食物。美国农业部气候变化项目办公室的科学家\n玛格丽特·沃尔什说：“即便整体粮食产量可能不受影响，粮食安全仍可能受到冲击。”\n【改写】some people may not have adequate access to certain foods ↔\nsome people may struggle to get the same access to certain foods\n“难以获取”改写为“获取不足”\nOne consequence of climate change ↔ food security can still be impacted\n气候变化导致的后果\n【辨邻】 H H 段 those same price jumps will likely cause certain foods to go extinct from\ntheir diets 是“因高价被迫放弃”，比 36 题更具体、主体限定为穷人，不等于泛指的“获\n取不充分”。\nfor poorer people, those same price jumps will likely cause certain foods to go extinct from\ntheir diets\nG G 段 High quality chocolate will be less available in the future 只针对优质巧克\n力，覆盖面窄，不承载 36 题泛指的“某些人对某些食物获取不足”。\nHigh quality chocolate will be less available in the future, and if you want it, you'll have to pay a lot\nmore for it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "People around the world are eating foods more similar than what they used to eat.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】全球饮食比过去更相似\n【定位】Jarvis and his colleagues also found that, over the past five decades, the world has seen an\nincreasing standardisation of diets; the foods we eat globally today are 36% more similar than\nthey were in 1961.\n贾维斯及其同事还发现，在过去五十年里，全球饮食日益标准化；如今我们在全球范围内所\n吃的食物，比 1961 年相似了 36%。\n【改写】eating foods more similar than what they used to eat ↔\nthe foods we eat globally today are 36% more similar than they were in 1961\n“比从前更相似”原义复现\nPeople around the world ↔ the foods we eat globally\n“全世界的人”对应“全球”\n【辨邻】 C C 段 no evidence that major food types like beans, chocolate, wine, corn or\nwheat will cease to exist 是“不会消失”，与“饮食趋同”不是一回事。\nthere is no evidence that major food types like beans, chocolate, wine, corn or wheat will cease\nto exist", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A recent news report talked about scientific efforts to help crops survive droughts through genetic engineering.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近期新闻报道助作物抗旱\n【定位】Last week, BBC Future explored one scientist's efforts to help crops cope with the increased\nprobability of droughts. By using the genes from resurrection(复活) plants, Jill Farrant of the\nUniversity of Cape Town is exploring whether she can design crops to survive for much\nlonger periods without water.\n上周，BBC Future 探讨了一位科学家帮助作物应对干旱概率上升的努力。开普敦大学的吉\n尔·法兰特正利用复活植物的基因，研究能否设计出能在更长时间无水状态下存活的作物。\n【改写】A recent news report talked about scientific efforts ↔\nLast week, BBC Future explored one scientist's efforts\n“近期新闻报道”=“上周 BBC Future 探讨”\nhelp crops survive droughts through genetic engineering ↔\nBy using the genes from resurrection(复活) plants, Jill Farrant of the University of Cape Town\nis exploring whether she can design crops to survive\n“基因工程助作物抗旱”=“用基因设计耐旱作物”\n【辨邻】 J J 段 a number of companies, organisations and researchers are aiming to create\ndrought and temperature-resistant crops through genetic engineering 是泛述，\n没有“某媒体近期报道某一位科学家”这一独特来源，故非 38 题源头。\na number of companies, organisations and researchers are aiming to create drought and\ntemperature-resistant crops through genetic engineering", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "It is predicted that climate change will affect the availability and price of quality chocolate.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】优质巧克力的供应与价格\n【定位】\"High quality chocolate will be less available in the future, and if you want it, you'll have to\npay a lot more for it,\" Jarvis says.\n贾维斯说：“未来优质巧克力会更难买到，如果你想要，就得花多得多的钱。”\n【改写】affect the availability and price of quality chocolate ↔\nHigh quality chocolate will be less available in the future, and if you want it, you'll have to pay\na lot more for it\n“可获得性与价格”=“更难买到+多付钱”\nIt is predicted that climate change will affect ↔\nclimate change will likely have \"great impacts\" on cocoa(可可粉) production in West Africa\n气候变化的预测性影响\n【辨邻】 H H 段 some foods will simply cost them more in the future 是泛指，缺“优质巧克\n力”这一专名锚点，故非 39 题源头。\nsome foods will simply cost them more in the future\nC C 段 beans, chocolate, wine, corn or wheat will cease to exist 里 chocolate 只用\n于“不会消失”的列举，不涉及价格与可获得性。\nthere is no evidence that major food types like beans, chocolate, wine, corn or wheat will cease\nto exist", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "People wonder if certain crops and foodstuffs could disappear like some animal species due to climate change.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作物会否像动物物种般消失\n【定位】Earlier this year, scientists warned that one in six animal species could go extinct(灭绝的)\ndue to climate change. Could the same thing happen to our crops and other foodstuffs too?\n今年早些时候，科学家警告说，由于气候变化，六分之一的动物物种可能灭绝。同样的事情\n会不会也降临到我们的农作物和其他食物身上？\n【改写】certain crops and foodstuffs could disappear like some animal species ↔\none in six animal species could go extinct(灭绝的) due to climate change. Could the same thing\nhappen to our crops and other foodstuffs too?\n“作物像动物般消失”=“同样的事降临作物”\nPeople wonder if ↔ Could the same thing happen to our crops and other foodstuffs too?\n“人们想知道”=原文反问句\n【辨邻】 C C 段 will they survive climate change? 紧接着用 there is no evidence ... will cease\nto exist 作答；40 题对应的是 A 段提出“会不会像动物般消失”的发问，而非 C 段的\n结论。\nwill they survive climate change? ... there is no evidence that major food types like beans,\nchocolate, wine, corn or wheat will cease to exist", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Although farms in the US can move a bit northward, crop yields may decrease.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国农场北移但减产\n【定位】In the US—the world's largest producer of corn and soybeans—farms can move north to\nsome degree, Schlenker says. But eventually, yields will likely suffer because the soil north of\nIowa declines in quality—a legacy of glacial(冰川的) expansion.\n施伦克尔说，在美国——世界最大的玉米和大豆生产国——农场可以在一定程度上北移。但\n最终产量很可能受损，因为爱荷华以北的土壤质量下降，这是冰川扩张的遗留问题。\n【改写】farms in the US can move a bit northward ↔\nIn the US—the world's largest producer of corn and soybeans—farms can move north to some\ndegree\n“美国农场可稍北移”原义复现\ncrop yields may decrease ↔ eventually, yields will likely suffer\n“产量下降”=“产量受损”\n【辨邻】 E E 段 a steep decline after crossing the 30℃ temperature threshold 是温度阈值角\n度，缺“向北迁移”这一锚点，故非 41 题源头。\nstatistical studies that he and a colleague built of corn and soybean(大豆) production in the US\nshow a steep decline after crossing the 30℃ temperature threshold(临界点)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One possible solution to the food security problem is diversification of diet.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】饮食多样化解决粮食安全\n【定位】Finally, diversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other crops could\nalso help.\n最后，让我们的饮食多样化、远离对热敏感的小麦、玉米、水稻等作物，也会有所帮助。\n【改写】diversification of diet ↔\ndiversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other crops\n“饮食多样化”名词—动词对应\nOne possible solution to the food security problem ↔\ndiversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other crops could also\nhelp\n“一种可能的解决办法”=“也会有所帮助”\n【辨邻】 I I 段 over-dependence on a handful of staples leave us vulnerable 讲的是单一依\n赖的风险，而 42 题要的是“多样化作为解决方案”，落在 L 段。\nhomogeneity(同一性) and over-dependence on a handful of staples leave us vulnerable to threats", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It remains unsettled whether the global food supply problem can be solved by creating heat-tolerant crops through genetic engineering.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】基因工程培育耐热作物前景未定\n【定位】Like Farrant's work with resurrection crops, a number of companies, organisations and\nresearchers are aiming to create drought and temperature-resistant crops through genetic\nengineering and conventional breeding. For now, the jury is still out as to how successful\nthose endeavours will be.\n像法兰特对复活作物的研究一样，许多公司、组织和研究者正力图通过基因工程和常规育种\n培育抗旱耐热作物。目前，这些努力能否成功仍无定论。\n【改写】It remains unsettled whether the global food supply problem can be solved ↔\nthe jury is still out as to how successful those endeavours will be\n“仍未确定”=“尚无定论”\ncreating heat-tolerant crops through genetic engineering ↔\ncreate drought and temperature-resistant crops through genetic engineering\n“耐热作物+基因工程”近义对应\n【辨邻】 B B 段 is exploring whether she can design crops to survive for much longer\nperiods without water 是描述某人的探索，缺“the jury is still out”这一未定论锚\n点，故非 43 题源头。\nJill Farrant of the University of Cape Town is exploring whether she can design crops to survive for\nmuch longer periods without water\nK K 段 other strategies might also help in some places 讲的是现成技术，不是 43 题\n“基因工程能否成功尚无定论”，故非源头。\nUntil genetic engineering comes to fruition, other strategies might also help in some places", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Poor people may have to give up eating certain foods because of their high prices.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】穷人因高价放弃某些食物\n【定位】But for poorer people, those same price jumps will likely cause certain foods to go extinct\nfrom their diets.\n但对较穷的人来说，同样的价格上涨很可能使某些食物从他们的饮食中彻底消失。\n【改写】Poor people may have to give up eating certain foods ↔\nfor poorer people, those same price jumps will likely cause certain foods to go extinct from\ntheir diets\n“被迫放弃吃”=“食物从饮食中消失”\nbecause of their high prices ↔ those same price jumps\n“因高价”=“价格上涨”\n【辨邻】 G G 段 you'll have to pay a lot more for it 是普遍涨价，缺“穷人放弃（give up）”这\n一落点，故非 44 题源头。\nif you want it, you'll have to pay a lot more for it\nD D 段 some people may struggle to get the same access to certain foods 是泛指\n获取受损，不带“因高价被迫放弃”的因果，故非 44 题源头。\nsome people may struggle to get the same access to certain foods", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A number of existing farming technologies could be used to reduce the negative effect of climate change on food production.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】现有农业技术减负面影响\n【定位】\"Many places could benefit a great deal just by using technologies that already exist,\" Walsh\nsays. \"General farm management can go a long way toward easing changes.\"\n沃尔什说：“很多地方只要用上已经存在的技术，就能大大获益。”“总体的农场管理大有助于\n缓解这些变化。”\n【改写】A number of existing farming technologies could be used ↔\nMany places could benefit a great deal just by using technologies that already exist\n“现有农业技术”=“已经存在的技术”\nreduce the negative effect of climate change on food production ↔\nGeneral farm management can go a long way toward easing changes\n“减少负面影响”=“缓解这些变化”\n【辨邻】 J J 段 aiming to create drought and temperature-resistant crops through genetic\nengineering 是未来导向的研发，不是 45 题“已存在的现成技术”，故非源头。\na number of companies, organisations and researchers are aiming to create drought and\ntemperature-resistant crops through genetic engineering and conventional breeding\nL L 段 diversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other\ncrops could also help 讲的是饮食结构调整，非“现有农业技术”，故非 45 题源头。\ndiversifying our diet away from heat-sensitive wheat, corn, rice and other crops could also help", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is a reason for the author to be in the habit of saying \"busy\" when asked how he is doing?", "options": ["A) He just follows successful people's example.", "B) He is actually proud to be fully occupied.", "C) He thinks everyone should be devoted to work.", "D) He believes busyness ensures accomplishments."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is a reason for the author to be in the habit 问一个具体可在原文找到的原\n因，答案直接落在某句话里。\n【拆句】主干是 What is a reason，后接 for 引导的逻辑主语 for the author to be in the habit of\nsaying \"busy\"，再用 when 状语 when asked how he is doing 限定情境。结构清晰，无\n陷阱，只需读成“作者在被问近况时爱说忙，原因是什么”。\n【定位】Sometimes it's because I actually am busy, but other times it's because that's what I think I'm\nsupposed to say. That's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say.\n有时是因为我确实忙，但另一些时候是因为那是我以为自己应该说的话。重要人物就这么\n说，得到晋升的人就这么说。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThat's what important people say. That's what people who get promoted say ↔\nfollows successful people's example\n预判“效仿成功者的做法”与 He just follows successful people's example 完全吻合，\n故锁定此项。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文末尾反问 why are we so proud to talk about how busy we are 是带反思的反\n问，并非作者承认自己“为忙而自豪”，选项把反问当作者的真实态度，属张冠李戴。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未说作者认为“人人都应投身工作”；相反作者反思忙碌文化，everyone should\nbe devoted to work 在文中无任何依据，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明说 working long hours doesn't drive better results，即忙碌并不保证成就；\nbusyness ensures accomplishments 与原文意思正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why do we tend to think that busy people are of high status?", "options": ["A) Our status can be attributed to our social mobility.", "B) We hold the belief that hard work leads to success.", "C) Our own opportunity for success never comes easily.", "D) We find few people of high status have time for leisure."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why do we tend to think that busy people are of high status 问的是原文研究已\n经给出的解释，属可定位的明确事实。\n【拆句】Why do we tend to think that busy people are of high status 中 think 后接 that 宾语\n从句 busy people are of high status。tend to 是“倾向于”，不是绝对，读时不必纠结，整\n句即“我们为什么倾向认为忙人地位高”。\n【定位】In other words, the more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard\nwork, the more we tend to think that people who skip leisure and work all the time are of\nhigher standing.\n换句话说，我们越是相信一个人靠努力工作就有成功的机会，就越倾向于认为那些放弃休\n闲、一直工作的人地位更高。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说我们对社会流动的信念影响了地位判断，并非“地位可归因于社会流动”；status\ncan be attributed to our social mobility 把“信念影响判断”偷换成“地位源于流动”，颠\n倒了关系。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe more we believe that one has the opportunity for success based on hard work ↔\nWe hold the belief that hard work leads to success\n预判的“努力工作通向成功的信念”与 hold the belief that hard work leads to success\n逐字对应，故锁定此项。\nC) 无中生有\n原文谈的是“相信有机会成功”，从未说“我们自己的成功机会来之不易”；opportunity\nfor success never comes easily 在文中无依据，属无中生有。\nD) 答非所问\n题问“为何觉得忙人地位高”，few people of high status have time for leisure 是描述\n高地位者的现象，没回答原因，且原文也未这么说，属答非所问兼无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about the culture of busyness from a recent study?", "options": ["A) It places employees in endless meetings, emails and stress.", "B) It compels some 20% of employees to appear always busy.", "C) It distorts many employees' belief of what a satisfying life is.", "D) It does much harm to many busy employees' well-being."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about the culture of busyness from a recent study 问一项研究\n的具体结论，答案在原文摆着。\n【拆句】What do we learn about the culture of busyness from a recent study：主干 What do\nwe learn，about 短语限定话题，from 短语点明信息来源。三段修饰平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】This culture of busyness is making it hard for employees to find work-life balance. According\nto a recent study, one in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout(精疲力竭).\n这种忙碌文化正使员工难以找到工作与生活的平衡。根据近期一项研究，每五名高投入员工\n中就有一名面临精疲力竭的风险。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nmeetings, emails and stress 出自上一段对“显得忙的人”的描写 knee-deep in\nmeetings, emails, and stress，并非“近期研究”的结论；题问研究发现什么，此项把别\n处内容搬来，属张冠李戴。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 one in five（约20%）指的是“面临精疲力竭风险”的人，compels some 20%\nof employees to appear always busy 把这20%偷换成“被迫显得忙”，数据所指对象\n被改。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未提忙碌文化“扭曲了员工对满意生活的认知”；distorts…belief of what a\nsatisfying life is 在文中找不到对应句，属无中生有。\nD) ✓ 正确\none in five highly engaged employees is at risk of burnout ↔\nIt does much harm to many busy employees' well-being\n预判“危害员工身心”与 does much harm to many busy employees' well-being 吻\n合，故锁定此项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do such utterances as \"I have limited access to email\" sound like according to the author?", "options": ["A) One is too busy to check all emails in time.", "B) One is opposed to the prevailing work culture.", "C) One is forced by circumstances to stop working.", "D) One is simply enjoying their time off work."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do such utterances as … sound like according to the author 问作者认为这\n类话听起来像什么，答案是原文作者的明确评价。\n【拆句】What do such utterances as \"I have limited access to email\" sound like according to\nthe author：such…as 举例结构，sound like 是“听起来像”。注意 sound like 后省略了表\n语，正是要补的答案；according to the author 提示以作者原话为准。\n【定位】Phrases like \"I have limited access to email\" and \"I'll respond as soon as I get back\" sound\nlike you're being held against your will from working as opposed to making the most of your\ntime off.\n“我邮件查看受限”“我一回来就回复”这类话听起来像是你被违背意愿地拦着不让工作，而不\n是在充分利用你的休假时光。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n题问这类话“听起来像”什么（即给人的印象），too busy to check all emails in time\n描述的是字面情境而非作者说的负面印象；作者的重点是“像被迫”，此项答非所问。\nB) 过度推断\n作者只说这类话“听起来像被迫”、设错了基调，并未说它表示“反对主流工作文化”；\nopposed to the prevailing work culture 是读者强行拔高，属过度推断。\nC) ✓ 正确\nyou're being held against your will from working ↔\nOne is forced by circumstances to stop working\n预判“被迫无法工作”与 forced by circumstances to stop working 吻合，故锁定此项。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文用 as opposed to 把“被迫不能工作”与“充分享受休假”对立，明说这类话听起来\n“不是”在享受休假；simply enjoying their time off work 恰是被否定的那一面，正好\n相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why did the author and his colleagues launch the Out of Office Email Generator?", "options": ["A) To enable busy employees to spend less time checking emails.", "B) To ensure employees as well as employers truly have time off.", "C) To stop managers from talking about how busy their teams usually are.", "D) To encourage both employers and employees to answer emails promptly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why did the author and his colleagues launch the Out of Office Email\nGenerator 问一个具体动作的目的，答案藏在原文相应段落。\n【拆句】Why did the author and his colleagues launch the Out of Office Email Generator：主\n干 Why did the author and his colleagues launch，宾语是专有工具名。一般疑问语序无\n陷阱，落脚在“为何推出这个工具”。\n【定位】That's why we recently launched the Out of Office Email Generator, a free tool you can use\nbefore your next long weekend or trip. You can share loud and proud that you won't be\nchecking email until you're back. Managers need to think twice about emailing their teams on\nthe weekend and talking about how busy they are. Leaders should take time off themselves\nand encourage employees to do the same.\n这就是我们最近推出“Out of Office Email Generator”这款免费工具的原因，你可以在下个\n长周末或出行前使用它。你可以大方而自豪地宣告，回来之前不会查邮件。管理者需要三\n思，别在周末给团队发邮件、别炫耀自己多忙。领导者应自己带头休假，并鼓励员工同样\n如此。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n“少花时间查邮件”只是手段、且只覆盖员工一方，原文目的是让双方“真正休假”；spend\nless time checking emails 把整体目的缩成局部手段，属以偏概全。\nB) ✓ 正确\nYou can share loud and proud that you won't be checking email until you're back ↔\nTo ensure employees as well as employers truly have time off\nLeaders should take time off themselves and encourage employees to do the same ↔\nemployers as well as employees\n预判“让员工和雇主都真正休假”与 employees as well as employers truly have time\noff 完整对应、双方都覆盖，故锁定此项。\nC) 以偏概全\n“别谈论团队多忙”只是原文对管理者诸多建议之一 talking about how busy they are，\n不是推出工具的总目的；stop managers from talking about how busy… 拿一个分支\n当全部，属以偏概全。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文呼吁休假时“不查/不发邮件”，answer emails promptly（及时回邮件）与原文意图\n正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What did the author's team conclude about the gender difference in airfares from their further investigation?", "options": ["A) It is largely attributed to women booking earlier than men.", "B) It is largely explained by women's choosing cheaper flights.", "C) It is mainly accounted for by male employees' readiness to pay more.", "D) It is due to the fact that women care more about their company's money."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did the author's team conclude 问作者团队得出了什么结论，是对文中已陈述\n事实的提取，不需推断。\n【拆句】主干是 What did the author's team conclude about the gender difference in\nairfares，特殊疑问句，conclude 后接 about 引出结论的对象；句末 from their further\ninvestigation 是状语，交代结论的来源，是关键定位信号，结构平铺、无陷阱。\n【定位】Further investigation allowed us to conclude that this gap is largely explained by the fact that\nwomen tended to book earlier than men, 1.8 days on average.\n进一步调查使我们得出结论：这一差距很大程度上可由女性比男性订得更早这一事实来解\n释，平均早 1.8 天。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthis gap is largely explained by the fact that women tended to book earlier than men ↔\nIt is largely attributed to women booking earlier than men\n选项 largely attributed to women booking earlier than men 与预判“价差主要因女性\n订得更早”完全吻合，是定位句的同义替换。\nB) 无中生有\n原文将价差归因于订得早，从未说女性“选了更便宜的航班”；women's choosing\ncheaper flights 是文中没有的新说法。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文成因是订得早晚，并非男性“更愿意多付钱”；male employees' readiness to pay\nmore 把订得晚导致票价高，曲解成男性主动愿意付高价。\nD) 张冠李戴\n“女性更在乎公司的钱”是第 4 段负向回报机制的延伸，不是本题进一步调查的结论；\nwomen care more about their company's money 张冠李戴到本题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What did the researchers want to determine by testing a variety of possible explanations?", "options": ["A) What made male frequent travelers book air tickets late.", "B) What caused women to plan ahead in booking business trips.", "C) What motivated women to book cheaper flights.", "D) What accounted for the gender gap in airfares."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did the researchers want to determine 问研究者想弄清什么，答案是文中明\n写的目的，属事实提取。\n【拆句】主干 What did the researchers want to determine，后接介词短语 by testing a variety\nof possible explanations 作方式状语，by 表手段；理清“通过检验多种解释”是手段、“想\n弄清什么”才是问点，避免把手段当答案。\n【定位】We wanted to determine what was causing these gender differences in booking business trips\nso we tested a variety of possible explanations, such as women choosing to plan ahead or\nmale frequent travelers being inclined to book late.\n我们想弄清是什么造成了订商务差旅中的这些性别差异，于是检验了多种可能的解释，比如\n女性选择提前规划，或男性常旅客倾向于晚订。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nWhat made male frequent travelers book air tickets late 取自文中举例的待检解释\nmale frequent travelers being inclined to book late，是被检验的假设之一，不是研\n究总目的。\nB) 以偏概全\nWhat caused women to plan ahead in booking business trips 同样取自待检举例\nwomen choosing to plan ahead，是子假设而非研究要弄清的总问题。\nC) 无中生有\nWhat motivated women to book cheaper flights 中“女性订更便宜的航班”文中从未\n出现，价差源于订得早而非选便宜航班。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhat was causing these gender differences in booking business trips ↔\nWhat accounted for the gender gap in airfares\nWhat accounted for the gender gap in airfares 正是预判“弄清性别价差成因”的同义\n表达，锁定该项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What happened when the negative reciprocity variable was taken into account?", "options": ["A) Both men and women were found to engage in negative behaviors.", "B) Neither men nor women viewed booking late as a bad behavior.", "C) The gender difference in airfare expenses no longer existed.", "D) The gender gap tended to narrow to a significant degree."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What happened when the negative reciprocity variable was taken into account\n问“当变量被纳入后发生了什么”，结果文中明写，属细节。\n【拆句】主干 What happened when ...，when 引导时间状语从句，从句用被动 was taken into\naccount；读懂“当负向回报这一变量被引入分析时”这个条件，结果句即答案。\n【定位】It only means that the gender gap disappears when we plug in the negative\nreciprocity variable.\n它只是意味着，当我们把负向回报这一变量代入后，性别差距就消失了。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文称男性比女性更常有此类行为 men tend to exhibit more of these negative\nbehaviors than women，并非“男女都有”；Both men and women were found to\nengage in negative behaviors 与原文相悖，且非“代入变量后”的结果。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说晚订不必然是异常行为 booking relatively late is a sign of abnormal\nbehavior 的免责说明，并非“男女都不视晚订为坏行为”，更不是代入变量后的结果。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe gender gap disappears ↔ The gender difference in airfare expenses no longer existed\nThe gender difference in airfare expenses no longer existed 与预判“性别差距消失”\n完全对应，锁定该项。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是差距“消失” the gender gap disappears，The gender gap tended to narrow\nto a significant degree 说成“显著缩小”，程度被改弱，与原文不符。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What did prior research on negative reciprocity among workers find?", "options": ["A) It can do more harm to the workplace than to employees.", "B) It contributes to the male-female divide in the workplace.", "C) It proves to be counterproductive in a number of ways.", "D) It can result in increasing labor-management conflicts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did prior research ... find 问先前研究发现了什么，是文中已陈述的内容，属事\n实提取。\n【拆句】主干 What did prior research on negative reciprocity among workers find，find 是问\n点动词；among workers 限定研究对象，prior 限定时间，定位时认准“先前的、关于工人\n的”那项研究。\n【定位】Prior research on negative reciprocity among workers found that it can result in lower\nemployee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气) and culture.\n先前关于工人负向回报的研究发现，它会导致员工积极性、业绩、职场士气与文化的下降。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未比较“对职场”与“对员工”谁伤害更大；do more harm to the workplace than to\nemployees 凭空造出一个原文没有的比较。\nB) 张冠李戴\n“造成职场男女分化”是后文 P6 对本研究结果的引申，不是先前研究的发现；\ncontributes to the male-female divide in the workplace 张冠李戴。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit can result in lower employee motivation, business performance and workplace morale(士气)\nand culture\n↔ It proves to be counterproductive in a number of ways\ncounterproductive in a number of ways 正是预判“多方面负面下降”的抽象概括，锁\n定该项。\nD) 无中生有\n原文后果是积极性、业绩、士气、文化下降，无“劳资冲突加剧”；increasing labor-\nmanagement conflicts 是文中没有的新后果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author emphasize about their analysis in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) It can help companies increase their savings significantly.", "B) It can duly contribute to companies' business performance.", "C) It can translate women's booking practice into men's behavior.", "D) It can enhance large multinational companies' competitiveness."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author emphasize about their analysis in the last paragraph 锁定\n最后一段、问其强调点，答案在段内明写，属细节。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author emphasize about their analysis，emphasize 后 about\n引出强调对象 their analysis；末尾 in the last paragraph 限定到最后一段，定位即在该\n段内。\n【定位】Our analysis suggests early booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year\nfor a large multinational company with 20,000 regular travelers.\n我们的分析表明，女性的早订每年可为一家拥有两万名常旅客的大型跨国公司带来一百万美\n元的节省。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nearly booking by women can translate into savings of $1 million a year ↔\nIt can help companies increase their savings significantly\nhelp companies increase their savings significantly 正是预判“早订为公司省大钱”的\n概括，锁定该项。\nB) 张冠李戴\n“提升业绩”是第 5 段先前研究提到的 business performance，不是末段分析所强调的内\n容；contribute to companies' business performance 张冠李戴到本段。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 translate into 的宾语是 savings（节省），不是“把女性的做法变成男性的行为”；\ntranslate women's booking practice into men's behavior 借同一动词曲解原意。\nD) 过度推断\n原文只谈为公司省钱，未涉及“提升竞争力”；enhance large multinational\ncompanies' competitiveness 是过度引申出的新结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
